            <ArticleTitle>[Arthritis due to Listeria monocytogenes].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>68, 70-1</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00393-007-0237-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 69-year-old man with liver cirrhosis presented to a surgeon, complaining of pain and swelling involving the right knee joint. A cloudy yellow synovial fluid was aspirated. The examination revealed polymorphonuclear leukocytes and Listeria monocytogenes was grown in the culture. The patient was treated with oral cotrimoxazole. Further follow-up was uneventful. The literature on listerial joint infections is briefly reviewed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
